course: dictionary
lesson: 2614
title: Dermal: to do with the skin
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Dermal is an adjective, a describing word. It means to do with the skin, so when you see dermal, think skin.
explain: The word comes from the same root as the dermis, the thick layer of skin under the top layer, which is the epidermis. Dermal is about skin, not about teeth, bones or eyes.
explain: Read this: «Shark skin is covered in tiny tooth-like scales called dermal denticles that help water flow smoothly.» The clues are skin, covered and scales, so dermal denticles must be scales on the skin. Check by swapping in skin: it still makes sense.
fact: Dermal means to do with the skin.
fact: The dermis is the thick layer of skin under the top layer, the epidermis.
fact: «Dermal» is a science and medicine word: dermal cells, dermal layers and dermal patches all relate to the skin.
fact: A dermal patch is medicine stuck on the skin.
fact: «Dermal denticles» are tiny tooth-like scales on shark skin that help water flow smoothly.
exercise: «Shark skin is covered in tiny tooth-like scales called dermal denticles.» What does dermal mean here? | To do with the water | *To do with the skin | To do with the teeth | To do with the sea floor
exercise: A doctor sticks a dermal patch on Sam's arm. What is a dermal patch? | *Medicine stuck on the skin | A dark spot on the sea | Sewing cloth for mending clothes | A map of the moon
exercise: The dermis is the thick layer under the top layer. What is the top layer called? | Dermal | *Epidermis | Denticle | Dermis
test: Which word means to do with the skin? | Dental | Skeletal | Ocular | *Dermal
test: «Some swimsuits copy dermal denticles to help swimmers move through water.» What do the swimsuits copy? | Waves | Jellyfish stings | Fish tails | *Shark scales that help water flow smoothly
exam: A doctor says, «The dermal patch goes on your arm.» Where does the patch go? | In your ears | Inside your heart | *On your skin | On your teeth
